  "text": "The Shunammite woman refused to leave without an answer. Second Kings chapter four. This woman is described as great, meaning of standing, and she is the one who notices Elisha passing through and builds him a room on the wall with a bed, a table, a stool and a candlestick. She asks for nothing. Elisha wants to repay her and she says, I dwell among mine own people. She is fine. He gives her a son anyway. Then years later the boy is out in the field with the reapers, says my head, my head, and is carried to his mother. He sat on her knees till noon, and then died. And watch what she does. She carries him upstairs, lays him on the prophet's bed, shuts the door, and saddles a donkey. She does not tell her husband what has happened. She rides to mount Carmel. Elisha's servant tries to intercept her, and she pushes past him. She catches Elisha by the feet, and when the servant moves to shove her away, Elisha says, let her alone, for her soul is vexed within her. And she will not go home without him. Faith can look like refusing to be managed.",
